---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Src from '../components/Src.astro';
import Aff from '../components/Aff.astro';
import { S } from '../data/sources.js';
const title = 'Can You Use a Virtual Mailbox for a Google Business Profile? What Google\'s Rules Say';
const description = 'No. Google\'s Business Profile guidelines say "P.O. boxes or mailboxes located at remote locations aren\'t acceptable" and that a rented mailing address you don\'t operate from is not eligible. What service-area businesses should do instead, and what staffed virtual offices and co-working spaces need. Checked Nov 27, 2026.';
const schema = {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":title,"description":description,"dateModified":"2026-11-27"};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} updated="2026-11-27" schema={schema}>
  <h1>Virtual mailbox addresses and Google Business Profile</h1>
  <div class="answer"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> No. Google's guidelines say "P.O. boxes or mailboxes located at remote locations aren't acceptable," and "If your business rents a physical mailing address but doesn't operate out of that location, also known as a virtual office, that location isn't eligible for a Business Profile" <Src href={S.gbpGuidelines} label="Google" />. If you work from home or travel to customers, set up a service-area profile and hide your address instead <Src href={S.gbpServiceArea} label="Google" />.</p></div>
  <p class="lede">A virtual mailbox gives you a street address, so it's tempting to put it on Google Maps. Google's own rules close that door, and even the mailbox companies warn against it. Here is what the Business Profile guidelines say, what to do instead, and where a mailbox still fits. Checked November 27, 2026.</p>

  <h2>What Google's guidelines say</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Address type</th><th>Allowed on a Business Profile?</th><th>Google's wording</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Virtual mailbox or PO Box</td><td>No</td><td>"P.O. boxes or mailboxes located at remote locations aren't acceptable" <Src href={S.gbpGuidelines} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rented mailing address you don't work from ("virtual office")</td><td>No</td><td>"that location isn't eligible for a Business Profile" <Src href={S.gbpGuidelines} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Office at a co-working space</td><td>Only with conditions</td><td>Needs "clear signage," must receive customers during business hours, and must be "staffed during business hours by your business staff" <Src href={S.gbpGuidelines} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Virtual office for a service-area business</td><td>Only if staffed</td><td>"Service-area businesses can't list a 'virtual' office unless that office is staffed during business hours" <Src href={S.gbpGuidelines} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your real location with a suite number</td><td>Yes</td><td>Put the physical address on line 1 and the mailbox or suite number on line 2 <Src href={S.gbpGuidelines} /></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Google also says businesses that show an address "should maintain permanent fixed signage of their business name at the address" <Src href={S.gbpGuidelines} />. A mailbox number at a shipping store or mail-scanning facility doesn't meet that.</p>

  <h2>The mailbox companies say the same</h2>
  <p>Anytime Mailbox's FAQ: "If you plan to use a digital mailbox for GoogleMaps, GoogleAdSense, or for a Google Business profile, it's best not to order one, as Google may not recognize the address as valid" <Src href={S.atmFaq} label="Anytime Mailbox" />.</p>

  <h2>If you work from home or go to customers</h2>
  <p>Google's answer is a <strong>service-area business</strong>: "A business that visits or delivers to customers directly but doesn't serve customers at their business address," like cleaning services or plumbers <Src href={S.gbpServiceArea} />.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Hide your address.</strong> "If you're a service-area business, you should hide your business address from customers," for example a plumber working from home <Src href={S.gbpGuidelines} />. Google: "If you don't serve customers at your business address, remove your address from your Business Profile" <Src href={S.gbpServiceArea} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Set service areas</strong> by city or postal code, up to 20, and keep the overall area within about 2 hours' drive of your base <Src href={S.gbpServiceArea} />.</li>
    <li><strong>One profile only.</strong> Service-area businesses "can only have one profile for the whole area that they serve" <Src href={S.gbpServiceArea} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Age-restricted products need a storefront.</strong> Alcohol, cannabis and weapons businesses aren't allowed as service-area businesses without one <Src href={S.gbpServiceArea} />.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where a virtual mailbox still fits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>On your website, invoices and business mail</strong>, so your home address isn't printed everywhere. See <a href="/how-to-write-virtual-mailbox-address/">how to write a virtual mailbox address</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>As your LLC's mailing address</strong>, separate from the registered office; see <a href="/virtual-mailbox-for-llc/">virtual mailbox for an LLC</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>If you need a staffed address</strong>, a virtual office with real reception is a different product from a mailbox. It still has to meet Google's staffing rule above. Compare them in <a href="/virtual-office-vs-virtual-mailbox/">virtual office vs virtual mailbox</a> and <a href="/opus-virtual-offices-vs-davinci/">Opus vs Davinci</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="meta">Google says it "determines how best to display your business address based on your business information and information from other sources" <Src href={S.gbpGuidelines} />. This page summarizes Google's published guidelines; Google makes the final call on each profile.</p>

